1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
 The borough mayor of Kourtinej, NBG, is back from maternity leave.

2
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:09,680
 She started a progressive return since May 6th after campaigning and winning the election while she was pregnant.

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,560
 Stephanie Valenzuela joins us in studio to talk about this transition and her priorities for the borough.

4
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:16,280
 Good morning. Thank you for coming in.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:17,560
 Good morning. Thank you so much for having me.

6
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:18,020
 How are you doing?

7
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:25,680
 I'm doing really well. I'm excited to be back and, you know, very, very happy with the team that we had

8
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:29,180
 that worked extremely hard over the last few months while I was absent.

9
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:35,700
 But we have, like, you know, very well, a lot of priorities that we need to work on and continue pushing

10
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,400
 so that we can deliver for the residents of Kourtinej, NBG.

11
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:39,280
 How's your daughter doing?

12
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:44,120
 She's great. She's four and a half months, but she's deep in her sleep regression.

13
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:47,120
 So we're lacking sleep at home. That's for sure.

14
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:53,280
 How are you balancing those responsibilities as a new mother and a new borough mayor as well?

15
00:00:54,060 --> 00:01:00,580
 Yeah. I don't think there's a perfect, you know, algorithm or equation that I can share that can work for everyone.

16
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:05,900
 What works for my family might not work for another family or for, you know, new moms out there.

17
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:15,100
 But I'm just trying to do my best to find the time that I need to address, you know, the important files in the borough and the executive committee

18
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:18,980
 and then try to really find time to spend with my daughter.

19
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:21,280
 And so it's a balancing act.

20
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:23,100
 It's something that I'm still trying to figure out.

21
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:24,380
 I'm fairly new at it.

22
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:24,680
 Yeah.

23
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:25,360
 So we'll see.

24
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,620
 How is it like taking a break from politics?

25
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:30,200
 Because it was a busy campaign.

26
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:30,980
 Yeah.

27
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:37,060
 I don't think you ever really take a break from politics, even if you are on leave, whether even you're on vacation,

28
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:42,620
 you kind of always have to be alert and aware or ready to get involved when you need to.

29
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:49,060
 So it was a good period for me in terms of trying to get disconnected from the world of politics.

30
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:52,540
 But there were a lot of different files that pulled me back in.

31
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:55,420
 And that's the world of politics.

32
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:56,400
 Like the Empress Theatre.

33
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:56,760
 Yeah.

34
00:01:57,060 --> 00:01:58,460
 We'll talk about that in just a moment.

35
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:03,020
 One more question on this, because I don't know how many people know this or enough people know this.

36
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:12,520
 But you, as politicians, as women in politics, you don't get the same amount of maternity leave or parental leave as everybody else.

37
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:12,880
 Yes.

38
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:15,120
 At the municipal level, we get 18 weeks.

39
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:19,240
 And after that, we have to return in some shape or form.

40
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:30,540
 And recently, actually, in the last mandate where I was city councillor, we were able to get a few, you know, advances in the treatment of parents in politics.

41
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:36,500
 So you have 18 weeks off and then you're able to return and you can do hybrid work.

42
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:39,260
 You can work virtually or you can work in person.

43
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:40,160
 It's your choice.

44
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,780
 But you do have to come back.

45
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:43,040
 It used to be 90 days.

46
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:43,720
 Yeah.

47
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:44,400
 Wow.

48
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:44,700
 Yeah.

49
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:49,120
 So there's been progress and there's a lot to work on still.

50
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:51,300
 But I don't take anything for granted.

51
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:54,360
 And I'm really privileged to be in the position that I'm in.

52
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:55,360
 And let's talk about that position.

53
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:56,620
 Lots to talk about.

54
00:02:56,760 --> 00:03:00,300
 Lots to do in Cotenej NDG over the next little while.

55
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:02,540
 What are the main issues you want to tackle in the borough?

56
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:06,040
 So we were really clear during the campaign what our priorities were.

57
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:07,560
 Housing first.

58
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:14,860
 Of course, you know, quality, basic services and security for all residents of Cotenej NDG.

59
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:23,420
 And so our team has been working extremely hard over the last few months with my colleague, Sunny Moroz, who was interim mayor.

60
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:26,180
 And I really thank him for his great work.

61
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,960
 And of course, the rest of my colleagues.

62
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:33,600
 But we've been working really hard to identify projects that we can push forward for housing.

63
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:38,000
 And of course, you know, everybody's focused on the Numer Hippodrome side, the old blue bonnet.

64
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,580
 So there's great potential there.

65
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:41,480
 And we're really advancing on that file.

66
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:47,780
 And, you know, there's also we made announcements in terms of infrastructure, sports infrastructure.

67
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:57,040
 We have 25 million that we're pushing just this summer for the redoing of the chalet parks in Trenholm Park, for example, Loyola Park.

68
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:01,980
 There's also Jean Pignon in the Cotenej district that is receiving tons of investment.

69
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:03,820
 And this is just the beginning.

70
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:06,800
 You know, we're six months more or less into our mandate.

71
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:18,720
 And we are really pushing to make sure that Cotenej NDG can be an affordable neighborhood that can really, you know, home and house families for years and generations to come.

72
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:22,180
 I want to bring you back to the area of Numer Hippodrome.

73
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:26,340
 And I talk about Cavendish and the extension of Cavendish.

74
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:28,400
 Where do things stand on that conversation?

75
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:36,560
 So my colleague, Alan D'Souza, who's responsible at the executive committee for infrastructure, is really working on this file at this moment.

76
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,420
 I don't have any news to announce for that.

77
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:41,840
 And I'm sure he's going to get mad at me if I say anything about it.

78
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,680
 He would love to do the announcement on that.

79
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:45,420
 Because he's been in that file for a while.

80
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:45,900
 Yes.

81
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,580
 So he's really he's prioritized it.

82
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:50,460
 He's really pushing for it.

83
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:55,120
 And I hope in the coming months we have something concrete to announce.

84
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:57,180
 But we are taking this file extremely seriously.

85
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:06,680
 And specifically on Numer Hippodrome in that area, in terms of housing, their housing groups, for example, have been pushing your administration, previous administrations to get moving, get a move.

86
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:08,900
 The land is there.

87
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:10,240
 The ideas are there.

88
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:11,040
 Let's get moving.

89
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,920
 How far along is that?

90
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:14,920
 Well, I mean, I just want to commend them.

91
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:15,500
 Rightly so.

92
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:24,140
 They should be pushing every single administration, whether it was us or the previous administration, to get some sort of progress on that land that does belong to the city of Montreal since 2017.

93
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:38,580
 So in terms of the Numer Hippodrome site, there was news that came out a few months ago about the Gallo receiving specific powers through a project of a law in the provincial government, for example.

94
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:48,440
 So we're really advancing in terms of looking at innovative ways we can finally develop this site without, you know, bankrupting the city of Montreal in terms of the infrastructure that needs to go in.

95
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:48,600
 Right.

96
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:54,760
 There's millions, potentially billions of dollars that need to come in for water and for other infrastructure.

97
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:02,280
 So the Gallo is going to be responsible for the development of the Numer Hippodrome site.

98
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:03,440
 And I think that we'll...

99
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:04,320
 And Gallo is an acronym?

100
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:06,180
 It's the GROUP.

101
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:07,200
 I don't know the...

102
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:17,680
 But it's a combination of people from civil society, from the organizations, local organizations are also involved in it.

103
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:25,960
 Project Genesis is part of the table and they've given their, you know, their suggestions and recommendations and what they think should be developed on the site.

104
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:34,160
 But, you know, in the last mandate, we had also given a contract for development for the first phase with Espace La Traversée on the site.

105
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:42,440
 So there's, there's, we're moving forward and we're just waiting to finally put that shovel in the ground and say that we're really developing it, but it's coming.

106
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:42,800
 Right.

107
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:53,020
 Oh, we spoke to your colleague, Sonny Moroz, as you, as you pointed out, was, it was interim borough mayor about the decision to scale back plans to add parking meters on and around Sherbrooke Street West.

108
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:59,060
 What is the borough's general plan when it comes to mobility and stuff like that in Cotinage NDG?

109
00:06:59,060 --> 00:06:59,500
 Yeah.

110
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:15,840
 So when it comes to mobility and stuff like that in NDG, we're actually coming up with a plan soon that we're going to be working on to really integrate connectivity, whether it's for bike paths or for, you know, safety crossings that are important in our borough.

111
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:22,340
 So I think it's really important for residents and for Montrealers that are listening to understand the complexities of Cotinage NDG.

112
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:32,980
 We're one borough that is surrounded by other municipalities, whether it's Cotinage NDG, Hampstead, Montreal West, and our residents pass through their streets and they pass through our streets.

113
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:39,300
 And we have to have a really connected plan that allows for our residents to get from NDG to Cotinage or to other boroughs.

114
00:07:39,380 --> 00:07:39,680
 Right.

115
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:41,420
 So we're definitely working on that.

116
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:42,980
 It is a priority for us as well.

117
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,000
 And we hope that we'll be able to announce something very soon.

118
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:54,780
 What is your relationship with, for example, Hampstead or, I'll quote St. Luke, in terms of figuring out and being aligned when it comes to mobility?

119
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:56,780
 It's a continuous work.

120
00:07:56,920 --> 00:07:59,560
 We always have to be in communication and collaboration with them.

121
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,440
 There might be issues that we don't align with necessarily.

122
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:03,460
 And how do you get past that?

123
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:06,280
 Conversation, dialogue, I think it's important.

124
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:18,860
 That's why when I started this mandate, although I was very deep into my pregnancy, I made sure that I met with all of them before I went into my maternity leave or as many as my colleagues as possible.

125
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:32,420
 Whether it was, you know, the Montreal West new mayor, Mr. Jonathan Cha, who also has a lot of big projects that he hopes to bring to his municipality or my colleague, Mayor Stern for Westmount, for example.

126
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:34,820
 So there's a lot that needs to be done.

127
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:42,120
 There's a lot of new also, you know, mayors around the table that I didn't have relationships with before as a city councillor.

128
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:45,340
 So that's something we're also really working on.

129
00:08:45,340 --> 00:08:55,080
 Still on the question of mobility, as part of the review of some bike paths, the city plans to look at one on Tehrban in your borough.

130
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:59,720
 What should residents expect regarding specifically Tehrban and that bike path?

131
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:06,160
 Yeah, what they should expect is that we're really working on finding something that is long term as a solution.

132
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:13,440
 What we inherited as a reality from the previous administration was a lack of consultation, a lack of...

133
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:17,160
 That conversation had been going on since well before 2020.

134
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:23,560
 Yeah, but the way that it was presented was there was no real openness to see what can be modified, right?

135
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:26,680
 So I think it was really presented as a fait accompli.

136
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:32,940
 And that was the grievance that was heightened really by the residents, especially those that were directly impacted.

137
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:40,640
 If they lived on Tehrban and they were, you know, living the realization of this bike path, they just didn't feel heard.

138
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:54,340
 And so we're taking the time and we've taken the time over the last few months to really re-speak to all of the actors involved, whether it's like the institutions, the church, the schools, the residents that are lacking, you know, parking space, et cetera.

139
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:58,560
 But also to the bikers and to the pedestrians that do enjoy that bike path.

140
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:03,240
 So, you know, we understand that it's not an easy conversation to have.

141
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:05,000
 Is there a middle ground in that conversation though?

142
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:09,560
 I think there is a middle ground and that's what we're working on with our services and our engineers.

143
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:16,440
 We have concrete scenarios that have been presented to us and now we're just evaluating which ones we'll be putting in place.

144
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:16,940
 All right.

145
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:23,440
 You're also responsible for international outreach and attractiveness on the City of Montreal's Executive Committee.

146
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,980
 What are your thoughts there on what you hope to accomplish?

147
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:27,560
 Yeah.

148
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:32,900
 Well, I want to say thank you to Vanna Nazarian who has been taking over the file as well at the Executive Committee.

149
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:36,460
 My colleague worked extremely hard to deliver on our 100-day promise.

150
00:10:36,460 --> 00:10:52,260
 We brought all of the actors around the table, whether it was for international sports or for culture or for our university, you know, field, in order for us to see where can we collaborate together to really put Montreal back on the map internationally.

151
00:10:52,580 --> 00:10:53,280
 Where can we...

152
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:54,500
 Was it ever off the map?

153
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:59,540
 I think we became something called in French incontournable.

154
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:05,040
 We used to be incontournable where we were, you know, everybody wanted to come to Montreal.

155
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:06,800
 Everybody wanted to invest in Montreal.

156
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:17,300
 But in the last few years, there has been a little bit of neglect in terms of how we care and cater and, you know, attract international events.

157
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:19,860
 And I think that we just want to make it better.

158
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:24,080
 Like, we want Montreal to really shine in the way that it has always shined.

159
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:32,700
 And that's our goal is to find out how we can attract more great events and investments as well.

160
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:33,180
 Right.

161
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,560
 Thanks for joining us this morning.

162
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:35,560
 Thank you for having me.

163
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:36,520
 We will talk again soon.

164
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:41,680
 That is Stephanie Valenzuela, the borough mayor of Côte d'Énge, Notre-Dame des Grasses.

